## Why This Is Asked

Explores a practical, prefix-based data structure with duplicates, common in autocomplete and search features.

## Key Concepts

- Trie with per-node prefixCount and endCount
- Duplicate tracking via endCount
- Prefix-based queries in O(length(prefix))

## Code Example

```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.prefixCount = 0;
    this.endCount = 0;
  }
}
class Trie {
  constructor() { this.root = new TrieNode(); }
  insert(word) { let node = this.root; for (const ch of word) { if (!node.next.has(ch)) node.next.set(ch, new TrieNode()); node = node.next.get(ch); node.prefixCount++; } node.endCount++; }
  erase(word) { if (!this.exists(word)) return false; let node = this.root; for (const ch of word) { node = node.next.get(ch); node.prefixCount--; } node.endCount--; return true; }
  countWithPrefix(prefix) { let node = this.root; for (const ch of prefix) { if (!node.next.has(ch)) return 0; node = node.next.get(ch); } return node.prefixCount; }
  exists(word) { let node = this.root; for (const ch of word) { if (!node.next.has(ch)) return false; node = node.next.get(ch); } return node.endCount > 0; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize memory for large, sparse alphabets?
- How would you extend to support deletion of all instances of a word efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked

Streaming sliding-window max is a practical problem in real-time data analysis. It tests the understanding of deques, amortized analysis, and handling duplicates in a fixed-size window.

## Key Concepts

- Monotonic deque for candidate maxima
- Fixed-size sliding window via index-based eviction
- Amortized O(1) per push/getMax
- Duplicate handling via index tracking

## Code Example

```javascript
class SlidingWindowMax {
  constructor(K) {
    this.K = K;
    this.dq = []; // stores indices
    this.values = [];
    this.i = 0;
  }
  push(x) {
    this.values[this.i] = x;
    while (this.dq.length && this.values[this.dq[this.dq.length - 1]] <= x) {
      this.dq.pop();
    }
    this.dq.push(this.i);
    if (this.dq[0] <= this.i - this.K) {
      this.dq.shift();
    }
    this.i++;
  }
  getMax() {
    return this.dq.length ? this.values[this.dq[0]] : null;
  }
  size() {
    return Math.min(this.i, this.K);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you modify for a variable window size?
- How would you adapt to support getMin() alongside getMax()?

